  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM EST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nToday-Fri...A weak cool front pushing into the southeast U.S. \nwill shift the Atlc ridge axis across our area and produce a light\nwind flow. E/NE winds today of 5-10 mph become more variable\nFriday with an afternoon sea breeze developing along the coast.\nMostly rain-free conditions forecast through late week though\nisolated showers should develop over interior sections each\nafternoon. The low rain chances will help floodwaters to gradually\nrecede across southern Brevard County and the Treasure Coast. \nHowever, residents and visitors should continue to avoid any \nflooded roadways and remain out of any lingering floodwaters. \n\nHighs will remain warmer than normal in the mid 80s today and\nFriday with peak heat indices in the lower 90s. \n\nSat-Wed...A weak cool front is still forecast to push through \ncentral Florida Saturday. Limited moisture with this boundary\nmeans it will likely be a dry frontal passage. A wind shift out \nof the N/NW will announce its passage. Winds gradually veer N/NE\nSun and NE Mon as high pressure builds in behind this boundary.\nThe GFS has backed off on the amplitude of its cut-off low across\nthe deep South and now shows it opening up into a trough as it\ncrosses FL Wed. This is trending toward the ECMWF solution which\nboth now show low rain chances Mon-Tue (20%) with the best chance\nfor showers on Wed (30%). Given the weaker upper support, severe \nweather looks unlikely and currently do not have thunder mentioned.\nTemperatures trend cooler this weekend behind the front though Sat\nstill looks warm in the low to mid 80s. Near normal highs in the \nupper 70s/lower 80s Sun-Tue. Min temps forecast in the upper 50s \nNW of I-4 early next week with 60s elsewhere.",
